  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Describing words in Swahili come after the noun and take its prefix, so “good” is nzuri, kizuri, mzuri or wazuri depending on what you describe. This unit shows you how.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Chakula ni kizuri sana!", "base": "The food is very good!" },
      { "target": "Si mbaya.", "base": "Not bad." },
      { "target": "Ni mpya kabisa!", "base": "It’s brand new!" },
      { "target": "Kikombe gani?", "base": "Which cup?" },
      { "target": "Meza ni nzito.", "base": "The table is heavy." },
      { "target": "Bibi ni mpole sana.", "base": "Grandma is very gentle." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "adjective-agreement",
        "title": "Describing words agree",
        "body": [
          "A describing word goes after its noun and takes the noun’s prefix. You already know **mtu mzuri** and **watu wazuri**. With a ki- noun it becomes **kizuri**, and with vi- nouns **vizuri**. N- nouns like **nyumba** take **nzuri**.",
          "Some words never change: **safi**, **hodari**, **ghali** and **rahisi** are the same with every noun."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Noun", "good", "new"],
          ["mtoto", "mtoto mzuri", "mtoto mpya"],
          ["watoto", "watoto wazuri", "watoto wapya"],
          ["kikombe", "kikombe kizuri", "kikombe kipya"],
          ["vikombe", "vikombe vizuri", "vikombe vipya"],
          ["nyumba", "nyumba nzuri", "nyumba mpya"]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1, 2]
      },
      {
        "id": "big-small",
        "title": "Big and small in every class",
        "body": [
          "The roots are **-kubwa** (big) and **-dogo** (small): **kikombe kikubwa**, **vikombe vidogo**, **mtoto mdogo**.",
          "With n- nouns, **-dogo** becomes **ndogo** and **-kubwa** stays **kubwa**: **nyumba ndogo**, **meza kubwa**. On its own, **kidogo** means “a little”: **Nipe kidogo** (give me a little)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Safi!",
        "body": [
          "Young people in Kenya and Tanzania say **Safi!** or **Safi sana!** for anything good: a goal, a new phone, a plan for the weekend. It literally means “clean”."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Compliments about size",
        "body": [
          "In many East African homes, telling someone they look well-fed or have **put on weight** (umenenepa) is a kind remark that means they look healthy and prosperous. Being called **mwembamba** may come with an offer of more food."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
